Abstract = {Alkyltransferase-like proteins (ATLs) share functional motifs with the
   cancer chemotherapy target O-6-alkylguanine-DNA alkyltransferase (AGT)
   and paradoxically protect cells from the biological effects of DNA
   alkylation damage, despite lacking the reactive cysteine and
   alkyltransferase activity of AGT. Here we determine Schizosaccharomyces
   pombe ATL structures without and with damaged DNA containing the
   endogenous lesion O-6-methylguanine or cigarette-smoke-derived
   O-6-4-(3-pyridyl)-4-oxobutylguanine. These results reveal non-enzymatic
   DNA nucleotide flipping plus increased DNA distortion and binding pocket
   size compared to AGT. Our analysis of lesion-binding site conservation
   identifies new ATLs in sea anemone and ancestral archaea, indicating
   that ATL interactions are ancestral to present-day repair pathways in
   all domains of life. Genetic connections to mammalian XPG (also known as
   ERCC5) and ERCC1 in S. pombe homologues Rad13 and Swi10 and biochemical
   interactions with Escherichia coli UvrA and UvrC combined with
   structural results reveal that ATLs sculpt alkylated DNA to create a
   genetic and structural intersection of base damage processing with
   nucleotide excision repair.},
